{"findings":[
{"rule":"J1","where":"Deployment._configureModules (setup override)","severity":"HIGH","why":"The override restricts country 410 instead of 408 and never calls super or _restrictCountry(RESTRICTED). Country 408 is therefore not restricted, and wallet c, registered in 408 with a valid KYC claim, passes both isVerified and the country module check.","steps":"Setup runs the override, so only 410 is restricted. _mint(b, 100); then vm.prank(b); token.transfer(c, 10) succeeds, although c is in country 408."},
{"rule":"J3","where":"Deployment._configureModules (setup override)","severity":"HIGH","why":"The country module is bound to the compliance contract, but the country set is wrong: it holds 410 and lacks the required 408. The restriction is not real.","steps":"After setup, countryModule.isCountryRestricted(compliance, 408) returns false. A transfer or mint to a 408 wallet passes compliance.canTransfer."},
{"rule":"J2","where":"Deployment._configureModules (setup override)","severity":"HIGH","why":"Because 408 is not in the restricted set, updating a wallet's country to 408 does not stop it from receiving tokens.","steps":"agent calls ir.updateCountry(b, 408); then a1 calls token.transfer(b, 10), which succeeds."}
]}
